This maps squarely to the **ACL fault family** on router6.

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** The nftables `filter` table, `FORWARD` chain on router6 — the distribution router serving the 172.18.50.0/24 and 172.18.51.0/24 (Location B) subnets.

**Mechanism:** Router6's `FORWARD` chain contains three blanket DROP rules gated on `iifname "eth2"` — router6's backbone link (the r6_r3 segment to router3 at 10.50.1.0/24). These rules drop all forwarded TCP, all forwarded UDP, and all remaining protocols (including ICMP) that arrive from the backbone side. The only permitted exception is TCP/80 from 172.16.10.0/24 destined specifically to 172.18.51.1. Packet counters on the DROP rules confirm live traffic is being discarded (51 packets caught by the catch-all rule at time of inspection).

**Localization:** Fault is entirely on router6, eth2 (r6_r3 backbone interface). Traffic initiated by Location B hosts (pc15–pc18) leaves router6 via eth2 without restriction, but ICMP reply packets returning from the backbone are silently dropped. Traffic initiated from all other subnets (e.g., pc11 on 192.168.200.0/24) reaches router6 via eth2 and is immediately discarded before delivery to Location B.

**Root cause:** An ACL (nftables FORWARD chain) injected on router6 blocks all forwarded traffic ingressing from its backbone interface (eth2), severing bidirectional reachability between the 172.18.50/51.0/24 networks and the entire rest of the topology.

**Note on user report:** The reported PC15↔PC16 same-subnet fault does not exist — direct-IP pings confirm 0% loss; the symptom is purely cross-subnet.

**Fix:** Remove (or replace with appropriate stateful accept rules) the three `iifname "eth2"` DROP entries from router6's nftables `filter/FORWARD` chain: `nft flush chain ip filter FORWARD` or delete the offending rules individually to restore full transit forwarding through router6.
=== END ===